#. Tag: title
#: profiles.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Profiles"
msgstr "Perfiles"

#. Tag: para
#: profiles.docbook:3
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A profile is a collection of default settings and restrictions that can be "
"applied to either individual users or groups of users."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: profiles.docbook:6
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The KDE desktop environment as well as all KDE applications use a specific "
"<ulink "
"url=\"http://techbase.kde.org/KDE_System_Administration/Kiosk/Introduction#KDE"
".2"
"7s_Configuration_File_Structure_and_Hierarchy\">directory structure</ulink> "
"to lookup configuration settings and various other information. KDE knows "
"about "
"several locations for these directory structures. All the information and "
"<ulink url=\"http://techbase.kde.org/KDE_System_Administration/Kiosk/Keys\">"
"configuration settings</ulink> found in the various locations are combined "
"before they are used. <ulink "
"url=\"http://techbase.kde.org/KDE_System_Administration/Kiosk/Introduction\">"
"Lock down</ulink> features control whether settings in the user's home folder "
"($KDEHOME or ~/.kde) are taken into account or not."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: profiles.docbook:20
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A profile consists of a standard KDE directory structure in a custom location "
"with settings and information chosen by you."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: profiles.docbook:25
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kiosktool; lets you chose in which location a profile should be stored. "
"Information about available profiles is stored in the file &etc_kderc;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: profiles.docbook:30
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It is recommended to store all profiles under a single directory. In this "
"case "
"the &etc_kderc; file only needs to contain a reference to this single "
"directory "
"and KDE and &kiosktool; will automatically pick up all profiles stored under "
"this directory. See the <xref linkend=\"settings\"/> "
"chapter for more information."
msgstr ""

